WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.456
A control should explain how it changes
the failure pathway.

2
00:00:03.606 --> 00:00:06.209
Prevention reduces the chance of failure.

3
00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:08.663
Detection identifies a problem.

4
00:00:08.813 --> 00:00:11.138
Response determines what happens next.

5
00:00:11.288 --> 00:00:16.365
For a mixing failure, a justified design
or operating control may prevent

6
00:00:16.515 --> 00:00:18.179
an unsuitable condition.

7
00:00:18.329 --> 00:00:22.596
An alarm may detect it, but only an
effective response can limit the

8
00:00:22.746 --> 00:00:23.833
consequence.

9
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:28.164
The three blocks are complementary; none
is automatically adequate for

10
00:00:28.314 --> 00:00:29.487
every process.

11
00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:35.400
Our fictional action is to verify the
agitator-status indication and the

12
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.833
defined response to loss of mixing.

13
00:00:37.983 --> 00:00:42.463
Engineering owns the technical check and
operations owns the response

14
00:00:42.613 --> 00:00:44.149
procedure and training.

15
00:00:44.299 --> 00:00:48.544
The verification plan records the
challenge method and the expected

16
00:00:48.694 --> 00:00:49.776
behaviour.

17
00:00:49.926 --> 00:00:53.574
Quality oversight reviews the evidence
through the site system.

18
00:00:53.724 --> 00:00:57.820
Action closed should mean the intended
result was demonstrated,

19
00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.488
not merely that a document was drafted.

20
00:01:01.238 --> 00:01:04.523
Adding a control can introduce a new
dependency.

21
00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:09.473
An alarm may depend on power,
configuration, sensor health or a shared

22
00:01:09.623 --> 00:01:10.699
signal.

23
00:01:10.849 --> 00:01:14.412
If the alarm and controller use the same
failed sensor,

24
00:01:14.562 --> 00:01:17.015
apparent independence may be misleading.

25
00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:21.347
The diagram shows a common sensor feeding
two functions to make that

26
00:01:21.497 --> 00:01:22.713
concern visible.

27
00:01:22.863 --> 00:01:27.428
Examine the actual architecture and
challenge assumptions before taking

28
00:01:27.578 --> 00:01:30.138
credit for an independent protective
layer.

29
00:01:30.888 --> 00:01:36.712
After controls are implemented, evaluate
what risk remains and who can accept it.

30
00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:39.209
Record assumptions and review triggers.

31
00:01:39.359 --> 00:01:43.689
Quality risk management does not excuse a
practice that conflicts with

32
00:01:43.839 --> 00:01:45.375
applicable requirements.

33
00:01:45.525 --> 00:01:49.899
For the activity, select one failure mode
and propose a prevention,

34
00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:52.289
detection and response strategy.

35
00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:57.004
Then identify evidence for effectiveness
and one new risk introduced by

36
00:01:57.154 --> 00:01:58.246
your proposal.

37
00:01:58.396 --> 00:02:02.066
This turns a list of actions into an
auditable argument.
